in a previous video i embarrassingly  shared my horrible spending habits and i  revealed my seven dumbest financial  decisions that i made in my early  twenties but today i want to share with  you my seven smartest financial  decisions that i made these choices  whether simple or not have made a huge  impact on my life but make sure you  stick to the end because i have saved  the best one for last for those of you  who are new hello i'm jessie on this  channel we talk about ways to simplify  your life so that you can live life more  fully with less if this topic is of  interest to you make sure to subscribe  for more positive videos like these so  right off the bat the first one is goal  setting when i was in early college i  was in a very difficult period of my  life and in an attempt to find healing  and sort of to find myself i asked  myself questions that i had never really  asked before  like what do i want from life what do i  want from myself and what legacy do i  want to leave behind when i die  and it was asking these sort of  questions that led me to develop a major  goal that i wanted to have a paid for  house by the time i was 27. so  eventually when i met my husband and we  started dating i presented this idea to  him and it became our goal we actually  did achieve this goal but it was not  because i set this like big goal and  then all of a sudden it magically  happened rather what it meant is that we  were able to break this big goal into  smaller chunks but the big goal was  important because it gave us a direction  or a vision of where we should head in  this way we could work backwards and  develop yearly goals monthly goals and  even weekly goals and this became so  important because at the time both of us  had debt i believe that setting goals is  immensely important because this way  you're not just aimlessly doing things  you have a direction you're sort of  carving a path for yourself this way and  it doesn't always go the way that you  plan and there will be detours along the  way but the overall direction does not  change so really quick for those of you  who may not be natural goal setters or  goal setting just might not come  naturally to you i want to share with  you an acronym that may be helpful when  it comes to setting goals and that is  smart this stands for specific  measurable achievable realistic and  timely when it comes to the way in which  you set goals the how you do them is  actually very important if you would  like to learn more about how to actually  set goals check out my two videos that i  have linked in the description below  numbers two and three are school related  and that is that i went to a community  college for as long as possible and i  paid off my school loans as quickly as i  could i don't think that everyone needs  to go to college my dad went as far as  fifth grade that's elementary school he  has rental properties and he loves what  he does but i needed to go to college in  order to become a registered nurse and  while i graduated at the top 10 of my  class and i could have gone to those  prestigious colleges that my classmates  bragged about i just wanted to get my  degree and graduate as quickly and as  cheaply as possible so that i could  start working i took college classes in  high school so that i could graduate  early i took summer classes in college i  worked through college as long as i  could and i commuted rather than living  at the college it took sacrifice i drove  an hour and 10 minutes each way but i  made it work by audio recording the  lectures and listening to them while i  drove and between all of these things i  was able to graduate from college with  minimal debt and i was able to pay it  off quickly and i also graduated college  much sooner than most of my classmates  who graduated high school at the same  time that i did truly i don't think that  for my degree the hospitals that i was  applying for were hiring me because of  where i went to school they were hiring  me because of me so i considered this a  very smart move and number four is that  i invested in myself i specifically took  advantage of employment education i was  an icu nurse for a few years and for the  first couple of years i really just  focused on soaking up information but  after that i became a preceptor training  new nurses i became certified in certain  devices and procedures and i got my  certification in icu as well and even  though i'm no longer an icu nurse i  think that this was a wonderful choice  because if your employer provides you  with free means of getting training  credentials certifications etc it adds  value to you as an employee it makes you  more hirable and it gives you better  opportunities whether you want to  advance or in my case if i were to want  my job back for any reason it would be  much easier to obtain it because of all  the certifications that i have and not  to mention that it gave me little  bonuses and raises along the way so take  advantage of any free employment  education that you have available it can  not only add value to your resume but  maybe your paycheck as well before we  continue to the next one let me know in  the comments what is the smartest  financial decision you have made thus  far share with us in the comments so  that we can all learn from each other  number five is that i focused on  improving communication skills and  self-development i've touched on some of  my mental health issues that i had in  the past in previous videos but there  came a point in my life where i was done  being a victim i wanted change and i  knew that it started with changing this  my mindset and because i wasn't  surrounded by the most positive people  at that time i looked for that  positivity in books i have found immense  value from improving my communication  skills through books like crucial  conversations it has made me a better  leader and has helped me tremendously in  my job and personal life books such as  captivate and cues by vanessa van  edwards have also helped me improve my  confidence and body language and of  countless other books from spirituality  to finance to self-compassion books and  audiobooks have tremendously helped me  shape a more positive mindset and have  slowly transformed my thinking over time  if reading is currently not a habit that  you enjoy check out my video on how to  start a reading habit that i'll link in  the description below reading no doubt  can change your life number six is the  fire movement i am no longer using the  fire movement as a part of my daily life  and i share all about that in a previous  video which i can link in the  description but in the beginning it was  just the boost that i needed to quit my  horrible shopping habits when i  graduated from nursing school i had  major lifestyle creep and i accumulated  other debt besides the school loans and  the fire movement really motivated me to  get my act together sell my crap and get  out of debt it fueled that fire that we  needed to work like beasts to build our  first home as our general contractors  while both working a full-time job it  was just that slap in the face and  wake-up call that we needed to let us  know that our financial future was in  our control and that we could in fact be  financially independent someday if we  were willing to put forth the effort and  sacrifice to get there so while we did  not continue with this fast-paced hustle  culture these few years certainly  provided major change for our lives and  lastly the best financial decision that  i have made thus far is one that i am  still making one that is ever evolving  as i am ever changing  minimalism  it is not something that defines me but  rather a tool that allows me to live  life more slowly through minimalism i am  able to free myself from the excess  whether that be a chaotic schedule a  cluttered home or a cluttered mind  minimalism focuses on simplicity and  having more of what we love and what  excites us and less of those things that  add burden  as i near my thirties and i watch my  children grow  i am reminded that my time  is truly all that i have in this world  and it is passing before my eyes  i no longer want to waste this precious  time working overtime every week to  accumulate more stuff more clutter more  waste more stress to manage it all  i now choose to be free from the  constant desire to buy more stuff  and focus on slow intentional living to  find beauty  in simplicity so that is it for this  video these are my seven smartest  financial decisions that i have made or  am still making in my 20s if you have  liked this video please give it a thumbs  up and subscribe so that you won't miss  when i post a new video i thank you so  much for watching and i'll see you in  the next one bye for now

